    <section class="py-16 bg-white">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-5 lg:px-8">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-6">How we work with young people</h2>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-8">Teenagers need to be understood far more than they need to be instructed. The work usually runs on two parallel tracks: the young person's own sessions and regular parent consultations. For younger children, see our <a href="/en/services/child-psychology.html" class="text-primary hover:underline">child psychology</a> page.</p>
        <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
          <div class="bg-warm rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm mb-2">Their Own Space</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">The session belongs to the young person. The limits of confidentiality are agreed openly with both teenager and parents at the outset.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-warm rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm mb-2">Working With Parents</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Parents receive guidance on boundaries, communication and defusing conflict before it escalates.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-warm rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm mb-2">Emotion Regulation Skills</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Practical, age-appropriate skills for managing anger, anxiety and overwhelming feelings.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-warm rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm mb-2">Liaison With School</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Where helpful and with family consent, we coordinate with the school's guidance service.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="py-16 bg-warm">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-5 lg:px-8">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-6">How do I know if my teenager needs therapy?</h2>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-6">Fluctuation is ordinary in adolescence. What distinguishes difficulty is duration and disruption to daily life. If a change has lasted several weeks and is affecting school, friendships or sleep, it is worth talking.</p>
        <ul class="space-y-3 text-ink-muted">
          <li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-primary shrink-0 mt-2.5"></span>A sudden and sustained drop in school performance</li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-primary shrink-0 mt-2.5"></span>Marked withdrawal from friends or from family</li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-primary shrink-0 mt-2.5"></span>Ongoing change in sleep and appetite</li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-primary shrink-0 mt-2.5"></span>Intense <a href="/en/blog/coping-with-exam-anxiety.html" class="text-primary underline hover:no-underline">exam anxiety</a> or performance anxiety</li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-primary shrink-0 mt-2.5"></span>Self-harm, or remarks that hint at it</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="py-16 bg-white">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-5 lg:px-8">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-6">What if my teenager does not want to come?</h2>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">This comes up often. Bringing someone by force generally does not work; a young person needs to see the process as their own choice. As a first step, a meeting with parents alone is possible.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">In that meeting we talk about how things are at home and plan together how to approach your teenager. Some processes continue entirely through parent meetings.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="py-16 bg-warm">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-5 lg:px-8">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-6">Will I be told what my teenager says in sessions?</h2>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">What a young person says in session is confidential. That is what makes the work possible; a teenager does not open up where they do not feel safe. Parents receive general information about the process and the areas being worked on, not the content of sessions.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">The one exception is safety. Where there is serious risk to the young person or to someone else, that is shared. This boundary is stated openly to the young person from the start.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
